WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.940
I am the benefits delivery and
decision transformation manager

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.080
for the California
Department of Transportation.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.480
Previously, I was the
data science manager

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.063
for the City of Los Angeles, as
well as at the Center for Data Science

00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:17.640
and Public Policy at the
University of Chicago.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.150
Mostly about data,

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.350
tacos, Los Angeles.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.940
Sunset sunset.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.020
Photos some architecture.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.960
All fun times.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.290
So here's the existential
panic that wakes me up

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.580
in the morning.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.280
50% of greenhouse gas
emissions in California

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.980
are currently caused
by transportation.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.380
Your state might be slightly
different or country,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.690
but it's going to be
in the same ballpark.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.210
If you've been paying
attention to the GHG world

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.940
at all over the last decade
and climate change and whatnot,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.690
you will know there's
this big transition.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.730
That was not true in 2010.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.180
In 2010, the biggest
emitter in California

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.940
was power generation.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.220
That sector is very
quickly decarbonizing.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.620
You can thank wind and
solar being really cheap.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.870
So you're like, great Hunter
buy everybody in electric car.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.100
We got this solved, right?

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.730
And the answer is you are wrong.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.000
I apologize.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.800
But if you look at the
lifetime total carbon emissions

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.140
of transportation, electric
cars are only about 50% better.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.700
And maybe, if heavy
industry becomes greener,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.070
maybe they'll be
about 75% better

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:29.120
than conventional
gasoline driven cars.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.070
Totally fine.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.240
This happens.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.980
We have a plan
for this, which is

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:37.690
we have a net zero by
2050 plan in California.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.230
The idea is California needs
to have a massive reduction

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.522
in something called VMT,
which you hear thrown around

00:01:43.522 --> 00:01:44.230
in those stories.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.440
It stands for vehicles, miles
traveled. Transportation world,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.580
much like technology world,
filled with acronyms.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.380
I'm going to try to only
use the tech acronyms today.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.820
So that gets us there.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.970
And then so California,
we wrote all these plans

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.180
and we established that we have
to double our transit mode

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.690
share in California by 2030 and
then we need to 5 exit by 2050.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.060
And you're like, great,
how do I take the bus?

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.800
So looking at this, I was
actually going to ask,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.120
show of hands who like
regularly takes public transit?

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.940
This is more than a lot
of talks I've given.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.720
Who takes public
transit and pays cash?

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.500
Okay. Who has like some
sort of weird, smart card

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.820
that they have to load?

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.830
OK, mostly smart
card users. Who gets

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.900
to just use normal
people money like you

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.340
would go to a coffee shop?

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.000
All right.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.940
Who who takes an agency that is,
like, super tiny? Like anybody

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.880
take Dial a Ride?

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.540
OK so long story short,
California, you might think,

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.920
is only three or four
transit agencies.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.400
You got LA Metro, you got BART.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.480
There are over 800 transit
agencies with over 200 agencies

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.030
offering fixed route
service in the bus.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.480
So when you think
about California,

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.640
Cal-ITP was founded
to answer this challenge,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.540
which is I want to go from
Santa Monica to Sacramento

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.230
or wherever, and I want to be
able to seamlessly plan, pay

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.780
for and execute my trip.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.440
We want to unify
transit in California

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.220
with a common fare
payment system.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.170
So you're not like
juggling all those cards.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.370
Anybody who got a smart trip
card for DC just for the week

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.280
can deal with that.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.300
Make sure that you have real
time data on where is the bus,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.080
and then make sure it's
seamless to get verification

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.980
of discounts and whatnot.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.118
I'm going to try to present
now that it is not as loading

00:03:44.118 --> 00:03:45.660
and we're going to
see if this works.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.650
Come on.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:54.330
Yes there we go.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.530
And all right.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:58.710
He does not like me at all.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.490
So we're just going to
keep going this way.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.258
So the things that we really
do to make transit seamless

00:04:04.258 --> 00:04:06.050
and I promise I'm going
to get to the R stuff

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.450
soon, unify the
customer experience,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.210
make sure that we
are prioritizing

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.520
sustainable modes
of transportation

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.450
and grounded in equity first.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:19.000
So how do you think
about a transit trip?

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.450
There are three, three
parts of the trip, right?

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.270
So the first thing
is, can I plan and get

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.090
to the bus or the train on time?

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.920
The next thing is,
once I get there,

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.820
can I pay for it successfully?

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.522
And then third in
the United states,

00:04:32.522 --> 00:04:34.730
we have a ton of different
types of transit discounts

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:35.370
and benefits.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.310
You might be a senior,
you might be a student,

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.600
your employer might
pay for your trips.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.360
There's all sorts of things.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.890
If you think about
California, it is big.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.130
There are tons of
like legacy systems

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.110
that you have to
go interface with.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.890
So we have a philosophy of
like transit technology needs

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:52.890
to be like LEGOs.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.400
You can snap your fare payment
system into your real time

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.310
passenger information system,
into your benefit system,

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:02.720
into all your different cad,
AVL subsystems and whatnot.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.990
We call a lot of this work on
the data front mobility service

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.970
data, which is all
about providing.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:10.520
So now we're going to
wonk out on trip planning,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.060
providing accurate and complete
information for trip planning.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.920
So, when you think like what
is the vision for that?

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:22.130
It's that you have a
complete and accurate picture

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.940
of what is going on at
any of those 800 agencies

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.000
in California.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.850
And then when I
pull up my phone,

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.670
whether I'm in Humboldt County
in the far North of California,

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.600
or Imperial County, down down
near the US-Mexico border,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.180
I could find where
is any bus, where

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.440
is any train, what are
my transfers, how much I

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.190
am I going to pay

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.450
in fare? It should easily
show up on Google Maps.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.940
So the way we do this is we
establish clear expectations.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.540
We assess every
agency in the state

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:53.630
using automated technologies
to say, how well are you

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:54.420
doing this?

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.240
And then we provide
technical assistance

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:57.920
to go meet those goals.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.690
So what is the clear
expectation for California?

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.460
For those of you who
are transit nerds,

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.500
you may have heard something
called the General Transit Feed

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.510
Specification, formerly
the Google Transit Feed

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:09.510
Specification.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.260
This is the thing that
means Google Maps,

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.900
Apple Maps, Citymapper, transit,

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.460
your R applications can see
bus locations and bus schedules

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.410
in both real time and schedules.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.280
And then on the back
end side, we also

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.250
have a set of interoperability
principles for the vendors

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.740
that piece together these
different pieces of information

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.190
to work together.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:34.760
Assessing progress.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.940
This is what's really fun.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.550
We take a look at
all this data and we

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.240
produce in-depth assessments
for each agency being like,

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.990
oh, you know, you didn't
have trip information for 33%

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.483
of your buses in the last year

00:06:49.483 --> 00:06:50.900
in real time.
Something like that.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.800
We talk to them every year
doing like a collaborative list

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:54.530
of stuff.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:57.490
We produce monthly public
reports on public transit data

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.330
quality and public
transit quality.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.970
How how many jobs
are agencies serving?

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:03.140
How many folks are doing?

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.780
And we do this for every
one of the agencies

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.905
in the state that provides
fixed route bus service

00:07:06.905 --> 00:07:09.310
now. And then we do
night light and we

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.890
monitor this all
nightly to make sure

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:15.850
that when things break,
for example, right now

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.980
school year is ending, a
lot of transit agencies

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.630
are changing service.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.880
A lot of the smaller agencies
are not telling Google that

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.795
that service is changing, which
means if you show up to the bus

00:07:24.795 --> 00:07:26.920
and they're using an old
version of their schedule,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.170
you're going to miss your bus.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.390
So we really try to make
sure that stays on top.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.060
And then finally, we do a
lot of technical assistance

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.350
work across our whole program.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.582
We have a transit data
help desk, you know,

00:07:39.582 --> 00:07:42.040
which has been really focused
on getting demand-responsive

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.220
transit into GTFS.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.610
That's stuff like Dial a
Ride, paratransit, whatnot.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.580
We've built open source
hardware and software

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.880
to produce GTFS real time to
help agents, small agencies

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.650
in rural areas to
go do it who can't

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.420
afford more complex systems.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.320
We've produced playbooks and
documented transit stacks

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.380
for different agencies, and we
do interoperable development

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:03.070
work.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:06.740
All right, so magic universe,
we've done all this work.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.630
You've now shown
up, the bus is here.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:10.910
What's about to happen?

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:12.840
We need a new way
to pay for transit.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:17.120
So, you know, from what we say,
paying for transit should be

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:18.740
as easy as paying for coffee.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.790
Customers can instantly pay.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.520
Tap their bank
card, their Apple

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.010
pay, Google pay, their
mobile wallet and whatnot.

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:27.780
In order to do this, you need
three pieces of technology.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:28.863
One is called a fare validator.

00:08:28.863 --> 00:08:30.988
Second thing is called a
fare calculation software.

00:08:30.988 --> 00:08:32.760
Third thing is called
a payment processor.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.429
This is tied to the.

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.260
One of the things
I love about Cal-ITP

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.510
is we hate inventing
stuff, so we really

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.140
try to leverage
global standards.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:44.340
So this is how the
banking system works.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.805
I did not know
anything about how

00:08:45.805 --> 00:08:47.430
banks worked before
I started this job,

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:51.630
and now I know perhaps a
little bit too much so.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:55.350
But rather than try to invent
our own custom flavor of how

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.190
to cards, how do we take money?

00:08:57.190 --> 00:08:59.670
Do we need to set up
TVMs or retail networks?

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.500
We just said, how
can we make sure

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.420
that banks and standard credit
and debit cards can work?

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.070
So those three pieces of
technology that I mentioned

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.893
are over here and they talk
between the providers bank.

00:09:12.893 --> 00:09:14.310
So this is the
state of California

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.710
has banking
relationships and those

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.510
go talk to Mastercard
Visa it talks

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.310
to your customers
bank all of this

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.010
happens basically magically.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.320
This is called the
CEMV standard which

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.190
stands for Contactless,
Euro pay, Mastercard, Visa

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.680
and any transit agency in
the country can buy this.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.755
We've done standardized
procurement.

00:09:33.755 --> 00:09:35.130
Anybody who's a
government person

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.380
knows procurement is the
worst thing about government.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.120
Absolutely maybe hiring
is worse, but pretty bad.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.520
So we have what we call
the mobility marketplace

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.300
for purchasing these
types of solutions.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.660
And it's pre-negotiated
it's like a bench contract,

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:52.660
really easy.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.860
And then on a data side,
what my team can do

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.570
is because that data
is standardized,

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.240
the types of outputs
you get from CEMV banks

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.440
all have been they've
documented this.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:05.160
Yes, you have to transmit
all the data over SFTP,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.910
but it is standardized.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:07.900
It comes in every day.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:10.980
We can build merchant services
for these transit agencies that

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.560
helps them understand their
revenue per day, what type

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:14.800
of fares they're getting.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.560
Are people paying with cards
or digital wallets more often?

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.170
And that's really focused on
the transit agency's needs.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.590
Joining that data
with their GTFS data

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:26.010
upstream to say like
this route is doing

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.697
more rides, whatnot
in their. Flip side

00:10:28.697 --> 00:10:29.780
of the problem in America,

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:30.840
I'll talk about
this very quickly,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.640
but we need to make sure that
we cover the un and under-banked.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.830
We work with different
issuing partners.

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.430
It's really kind of crazy,
but it's much easier

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:39.340
to get accounts.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:41.173
This is sort of grounded
in our equity goal.

00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:43.132
I could talk for a much
longer time about this,

00:10:43.132 --> 00:10:44.790
but I'm going to kind
of speed through.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.970
I'm also going to speed through
this that discount enrollments,

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.010
you want to standardize
and simplify.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.310
We have a web application.

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:52.852
If this was a web
conference, I would

00:10:52.852 --> 00:10:55.890
talk about how we do all this
non PII linking of people's

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:57.875
identities to stuff.

00:10:57.875 --> 00:10:59.500
This is what it looks
like in practice.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.940
This is the
Sacramento light rail.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:02.230
You don't need a special card.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.270
You can just go and tap your
credit and debit card.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:05.350
It works really easily.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:06.960
You get fare capping.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.460
So this is like the
fun data nerdery part.

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.880
We have 800 transit
agencies we're stewarding,

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.640
like that is too many
to manage ETL processes

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.330
for each of those agencies.

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:20.610
So what we focus on is what we
call our core data sets, which

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:24.600
is we use these three data
standards to really drive

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:25.990
our analytics processes.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:29.220
So GTFS schedule, we
do that every night.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:33.090
We do all sorts of different
output analysis from that data.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.933
GTFS real time, again, this
is just, where is the bus

00:11:35.933 --> 00:11:37.600
and when is it going
to show up at stop.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.147
We get that data
every 20 seconds.

00:11:39.147 --> 00:11:41.730
This is sort of actually gets
to be big data because you start

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.940
talking about the bus location
of every bus in California,

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.740
every 20 seconds.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:50.170
You can do that to do level of
delay analysis, speed analysis.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:53.910
Where should how
can you optimize

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:56.730
where to place potential
infrastructure investments?

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.230
And then finally,
our third standard

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.180
EMV payments two days
after the transaction

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.600
